            <div xml:id="d179">

                <pb facs="0204" n="192" sameAs="frus:frus1942v05/pg192"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">862.20210/1197: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Brazil</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Caffery</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Rio de
                                Janeiro</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1942-03-31T18:00:00-03:00">March 31, 1942—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 8:25 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1104. Department’s 808, March 28, noon.<anchor n="13" xml:id="d179-f1"/> Since
                    the São Paulo police have seized the code used by N. C. Christensen (Lucas) they
                    are of course able to decode some of the intercepted messages. The Brazilian
                    Government intercepted a great many messages but was formerly unable to read
                    them.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Caffery</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
